# Collections: J. R. R. Tolkien. Tree and leaf, The adventures of Tom Bombadil and Tales from the
# perilous realm are named collections, modelled as works with parts (the last is a publisher's
# selection, which is open to discussion); the poems of Tom Bombadil are in two of the publications. The two Unwin books of 1975
# and HarperCollins' boxed sets of The hobbit and The lord of the rings (2011) and of The lord of the
# rings with Hammond and Scull's Reader's companion (2005, 2014) have no work for the whole; the companion
# is a work of its own, a commentary on The lord of the rings. The first two have no collective title:
# the manifestation embodies the expressions, and there is no work for the whole. The lord of the
# rings is one work with its three volumes as parts, and has expressions for the volumes only. A manifestation embodies the expression of a collection and of everything in it.
# Publication data from the MARC 21 records in swib26/marc/CollectionsTolkien.xml.

